.TH UNAME 1 "September 2026" "mitos-utils 0.1.0" "MITOS User Commands"
.SH NAME
uname \- print system information
.SH SYNOPSIS
.B uname
[\fB\-a\fR|\fB\-s\fR|\fB\-n\fR|\fB\-r\fR|\fB\-m\fR]
.SH DESCRIPTION
Print system information. With no options, or with \fB\-a\fR, prints kernel name, hostname, kernel release, version, and machine hardware name.
.SH OPTIONS
.TP
.B \-a, \-\-all
Print all fields (the default).
.TP
.B \-s, \-\-kernel\-name
Print the kernel name only.
.TP
.B \-n, \-\-nodename
Print the network node hostname only.
.TP
.B \-r, \-\-kernel\-release
Print the kernel release only.
.TP
.B \-m, \-\-machine
Print the machine hardware name only.
.SH GLOBAL OPTIONS
Every mitos-utils command additionally accepts \fB\-\-help\fR (print a usage line and exit) and \fB\-\-version\fR. Commands that take file/path arguments also accept \fB\-\-\fR to mark the end of options, so a file literally named e.g. \fB\-oddfile\fR can still be referenced.
.SH EXIT STATUS
0 on success.
.SH SEE ALSO
.BR mitos\-box (1),
.BR mitos\-utils\-compat (7),
.BR mitos\-utils\-integration (7)
